/**
@page quickstart Quickstart

@brief Integrate the library into an Arduino PLC IDE project.

This walks through delivering Botanik into an Axel PLC IDE project: vendoring the
sources into the sketch folder, wiring the entry point, and keeping the emulator
running against the same site code. It assumes you already have a site building
under the simulator (see @ref setup).

@section qs_why Why vendoring, not a library import

The Axel PLC IDE can only install libraries from a fixed, years-old public index,
so neither "Add .ZIP Library" nor a normal library dependency is available to us.
The delivery path is therefore to copy the sources into the IDE project's sketch
folder and reference them directly.

Three rules of the Arduino sketch build shape everything below. They were measured
against `arduino-cli` with the real `arduino:mbed_opta` core, not assumed:

| Rule | Consequence |
|------|-------------|
| `<sketch>/src/**` is compiled, recursively | the vendored tree goes in `src/`, and nowhere else - a subfolder by any other name is silently not compiled |
| No include directory inside the sketch folder is added - not the sketch root, not `src/` | every header must resolve *relative to the including file*; this is why libbotanik's internal includes and the site's are all relative |
| `-DTARGET_OPTA` *is* passed to every TU (it ships in the core's `variants/OPTA/defines.txt`) | HAL selection works with no help from us |

The second rule is the one that bites. A quoted include searches the including
file's own directory, which is why a `.ino` at the sketch root can say
`#include "src/..."` but a file *inside* `src/` cannot.

@section qs_vendor Produce the tree

@code{.sh}
make -C build vendor          # -> build/vendor/src/ + entry.cpp.example
@endcode

Platform-independent (it only copies text), so it runs from the default SIMULATOR
tree. The result mirrors our own `src/`, which is required: the site's includes of
the library are written against the `sites/<name>/` position.

@code
<sketch>/
  <sketch>.ino                  trivial: calls botanik_setup() / botanik_step()
  entry.cpp                     yours, from entry.cpp.example
  src/                          <- build/vendor/src, dropped in wholesale
    libbotanik.h  libbotanik_config.h  libbotanik_features.h  ...
    libbotanik/...              the library
    sites/<name>/...            your generated site
    botanik_site_config.h       generated hook, see below
@endcode

@section qs_config How the site config reaches the library

The site's `config.h` carries the build-wide `BOTANIK_*` overrides that
`libbotanik_config.h` must see *before* its own defaults - and the IDE gives us no
`-D` to point at it. So `make vendor` writes a one-line `botanik_site_config.h` at
the tree root:

@code{.cpp}
#include "sites/gh_mlb41/config.h"
@endcode

`libbotanik_config.h` picks it up through an `__has_include` hook. Because that
header sits at the same tree root, the quoted include resolves against its own
directory - so every library TU gets the site's overrides with no build-system
cooperation at all. Nothing to configure, and no way for one TU to miss it.

@section qs_entry The entry point

`entry.cpp` lives at the **sketch root**, not in `src/`: that is the one location
from which `#include "src/sites/<name>/site.h"` resolves. It constructs the
firmware as a function-local static (so it is built after the core's `init()` has
brought up the UARTs and the shared-memory region) and defines the two hooks:

@code{.cpp}
#include "src/sites/gh_mlb41/site.h"

static BotanikSite& firmware() {
    static BotanikSite fw;      // default ctor binds the PLC region via HAL
    return fw;
}

extern "C" void botanik_setup(void) { firmware().setup(); }
extern "C" void botanik_step(void)  { firmware().loop(); }
@endcode

The sketch window stays trivial:

@code{.cpp}
extern "C" void botanik_setup(void);
extern "C" void botanik_step(void);
void setup() { botanik_setup(); }
void loop()  { botanik_step(); }
@endcode

@note The hook names `botanik_setup` / `botanik_step` are placeholders pending
confirmation of the symbol the Axel runtime actually calls into; adjust them to
match. This is the last unconfirmed piece of the integration.

@section qs_assert The payload contract

This is the check worth wiring first, because it is the one that catches a
regenerated PLC program drifting from the regenerated site. Once the PLC IDE's
generated types are in scope in `entry.cpp`:

@code{.cpp}
static_assert(sizeof(PLCSharedVarsInput_t)   == sizeof(Botanik::PlcIO::PlcInput<PlcInputData_t>), "");
static_assert(sizeof(PLCSharedVarsOutput_t)  == sizeof(Botanik::PlcIO::PlcOutput<PlcOutputData_t>), "");
static_assert(alignof(PLCSharedVarsOutput_t) == alignof(Botanik::PlcIO::PlcOutput<PlcOutputData_t>), "");
@endcode

@section qs_emulator Running the emulator on the same site code

The point of keeping the site in your project is that the simulator runs the very
same generated code. Point the build at the site directory inside your sketch:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B build-site -DBOTANIK_TARGET=SIMULATOR \
      -DBOTANIK_SITE_DIR=/path/to/LLSketch/src/sites/gh_mlb41
make -C build-site botanik_sim && ./build-site/botanik_sim
@endcode

The site owns its mock coprocessor (`Site::MockPlc` in its `sim.h`), so the
emulator is instantiated against your generated payload types - same layout, same
table offsets, same alarm bits as the hardware build.

@section qs_abi Checking the ARM build without the IDE

For a fast "does it still build and fit" check without the Arduino toolchain:

@code{.sh}
cmake -B build-opta -DBOTANIK_TARGET=OPTA
make -C build-opta
@endcode

This exercises size, LTO, and the footprint `static_assert`s. Use the IDE to
produce the actual flashable image.
*/
